    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Cloud Security and Identity Management', 'Qt9lhzFhW_c', '### Introduction
Cloud security works differently from a private data centre. The provider secures the infrastructure, and you must secure what you put on it.

### Key concepts
- **Shared responsibility model:** divides duties between provider and customer.
- **IAM:** users, roles and policies.
- **Encryption:** protect data at rest and in transit.
- **Network controls:** security groups and private networks.
- **Logging:** record who did what, for example with CloudTrail.

### Definitions
- **IAM role:** a set of permissions that can be assumed temporarily.
- **Policy:** a document defining what''s allowed.
- **Least privilege:** only the permissions required.
- **Misconfiguration:** an incorrect setting that creates exposure.

### Example
A public storage bucket is a common real-world mistake that has exposed private files. Keeping public access blocked by default and granting access through specific roles prevents it.

### Key takeaways
- Misconfiguration causes many cloud breaches.
- Use roles rather than long-lived access keys.
- Never commit cloud credentials to source control.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Cloud Computing Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cloud Security and Identity Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 8, 'Cloud Deployment and Practical Projects', '7Gym2XVcA5A', '### Introduction
Deploying an application is where the cloud concepts come together. A small real deployment teaches more than reading about services.

### Key concepts
- **Basic deployment:** launch a server, install the runtime, and run the app.
- **Static hosting:** serve a site from storage and a CDN.
- **CI/CD:** build, test and deploy automatically.
- **Infrastructure as Code:** define resources in files, such as Terraform.
- **Load balancing:** spread traffic across servers.

### Definitions
- **CI/CD:** Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.
- **CDN:** a network that serves content from locations close to users.
- **Load balancer:** distributes requests between servers.

### Example
Deploying a website on EC2: launch an instance, allow ports 22 and 80 in the security group, connect by SSH, install a web server, and upload your files. Then visit the server''s public address.

### Key takeaways
- Start by hosting a simple static website.
- Automate deployments once they work by hand.
- Restrict SSH access and delete practice resources when you finish.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Cloud Computing Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cloud Deployment and Practical Projects');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Business and Management', 'a-uPznyA7Hw', '### Introduction
A business provides goods or services, usually for profit. Management is the work of getting people and resources to achieve its goals effectively.

### Key concepts
- **Business types:** sole proprietorship, partnership and company.
- **Stakeholders:** owners, employees, customers, suppliers and society.
- **Levels of management:** top, middle and first-line.
- **Management skills:** technical, human and conceptual.

### Definitions
- **Management:** achieving goals through people and resources.
- **Efficiency:** doing things right, with minimal waste.
- **Effectiveness:** doing the right things, achieving the goal.
- **Stakeholder:** anyone affected by the business.

### Example
In a bakery chain, the CEO decides whether to expand (top level), a regional manager plans store targets (middle level), and a shift supervisor organizes today''s baking staff (first-line).

### Key takeaways
- Good managers need both efficiency and effectiveness.
- Higher levels need more conceptual skill; lower levels need more technical skill.
- A business must balance the interests of all stakeholders.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Business and Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Principles and Functions of Management', 'WC6u9EskeN8', '### Introduction
Management work follows recognizable patterns. Classical thinkers such as Henri Fayol described functions and principles that remain widely used today.

### Key concepts
- **Four functions:** planning, organizing, leading and controlling.
- **Fayol''s principles:** include division of work, authority and responsibility, unity of command, and unity of direction.
- **Scientific management:** Frederick Taylor''s study of work to improve efficiency.
- **The cycle:** controlling results feeds back into new plans.

### Definitions
- **Planning:** deciding goals and how to reach them.
- **Organizing:** arranging people and resources.
- **Leading:** motivating and directing people.
- **Controlling:** comparing results with goals and correcting them.

### Example
Launching a product: set a sales target (planning), form a team with clear roles (organizing), motivate them (leading), and check monthly sales against the target to adjust (controlling).

### Key takeaways
- The four functions form a continuous cycle.
- Unity of command means each person reports to one manager.
- Principles are guidelines to adapt, not rigid rules.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Principles and Functions of Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Planning and Decision Making', 'S0vKNan8YTs', '### Introduction
Planning sets direction, and decision making chooses among options. Sound planning reduces uncertainty and focuses effort.

### Key concepts
- **Types of plans:** strategic (long-term), tactical (medium-term), operational (short-term).
- **SMART goals:** Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound.
- **Decision process:** identify the problem, list options, evaluate, choose, implement, review.
- **SWOT analysis:** Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats.

### Definitions
- **Objective:** a specific result to achieve.
- **Strategy:** the overall approach to reaching objectives.
- **Programmed decision:** a routine choice with a set rule.
- **Non-programmed decision:** a new, complex choice.

### Example
"Improve sales" is a vague goal. "Increase online sales by 15% by December through a new mobile site" is SMART, because it''s specific, measurable and has a deadline.

### Key takeaways
- A goal you can''t measure is hard to manage.
- Plans should be reviewed as conditions change.
- Big decisions benefit from gathering data and other viewpoints.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Planning and Decision Making');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Organizing and Staffing', '5kh5VRG6Zfk', '### Introduction
Organizing divides work and defines who reports to whom. Staffing makes sure the right people fill those roles.

### Key concepts
- **Organizational structures:** functional, divisional and matrix.
- **Delegation:** assigning work along with the authority to do it.
- **Span of control:** the number of people a manager supervises.
- **Staffing process:** planning, recruitment, selection, training, appraisal.

### Definitions
- **Authority:** the right to make decisions.
- **Responsibility:** the duty to complete a task.
- **Accountability:** answering for results.
- **Recruitment:** attracting suitable candidates.

### Example
A growing company organized by function has marketing and finance departments. As it enters new countries, it may shift to a divisional structure, with one division for each region.

### Key takeaways
- Authority and responsibility should match.
- Delegation develops staff and frees managers for bigger issues.
- Hiring and training well are investments, not just costs.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Organizing and Staffing');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Leadership and Motivation', 'qzoIAJYPQwo', '### Introduction
Managers run processes, while leaders inspire people. Understanding what motivates people helps teams perform willingly rather than under pressure.

### Key concepts
- **Leadership styles:** autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire.
- **Maslow''s hierarchy:** physiological, safety, social, esteem, self-actualization.
- **Herzberg''s theory:** hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators create satisfaction.
- **Theory X and Y:** McGregor''s contrasting assumptions about workers.

### Definitions
- **Leadership:** influencing people toward a goal.
- **Motivation:** the drive that directs effort.
- **Intrinsic motivation:** enjoying the work itself.
- **Extrinsic motivation:** external rewards, such as pay.

### Example
Raising pay may stop complaints, but it may not inspire better work (a Herzberg hygiene factor). Giving recognition and more challenging projects is more likely to motivate.

### Key takeaways
- No single leadership style suits every situation.
- People are motivated by different things, so get to know your team.
- Recognition is powerful and costs little.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Leadership and Motivation');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Communication and Team Management', '4BO42rTCZS8', '### Introduction
Most management problems involve communication. Strong teams depend on clear information, shared goals and trust.

### Key concepts
- **Communication process:** sender, message, channel, receiver, feedback.
- **Barriers:** noise, jargon, assumptions and emotions.
- **Tuckman''s team stages:** forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning.
- **Conflict:** handled well, it can improve decisions.

### Definitions
- **Active listening:** focusing on understanding rather than preparing a reply.
- **Feedback:** information about performance.
- **Team cohesion:** how strongly members work together.

### Example
A manager says "get the report done soon", and the team assumes next week. "Please send the sales report by 5 pm Friday" avoids that confusion by making the deadline clear.

### Key takeaways
- Check that your message was understood.
- Give feedback that is specific, timely and about behaviour.
- Expect early conflict in new teams; it''s a normal stage.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Communication and Team Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Business Strategy and Organizational Growth', 'JVffzkcI02M', '### Introduction
Strategy is how a business competes and grows over the long term. It decides what to do, and just as importantly, what not to do.

### Key concepts
- **Porter''s generic strategies:** cost leadership, differentiation and focus.
- **Porter''s Five Forces:** analyses competition in an industry.
- **Competitive advantage:** what you do better than rivals.
- **Growth options:** new markets, new products, mergers and acquisitions.
- **Ansoff Matrix:** maps growth by products and markets.

### Definitions
- **Cost leadership:** competing by having the lowest costs.
- **Differentiation:** offering something uniquely valued.
- **Market share:** your portion of total sales in a market.

### Example
A budget airline competes on cost, with low fares and basic service. A premium airline differentiates on comfort. A company trying to do both risks being "stuck in the middle".

### Key takeaways
- A strategy means making clear choices.
- Growth should build on real strengths.
- Review strategy as competitors and markets change.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Principles of Business Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Business Strategy and Organizational Growth');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Financial Accounting', 'Rpa_UAciIeU', '### Introduction
Financial accounting records a business''s transactions and reports its performance to people outside the company, such as investors and lenders.

### Key concepts
- **The accounting process:** identify, record, classify, summarize and report.
- **Users:** investors, lenders, tax authorities and managers.
- **Main statements:** income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement.
- **Financial vs management accounting:** outside users versus internal decision makers.

### Definitions
- **Asset:** something the business owns or controls that has value.
- **Liability:** an amount the business owes.
- **Equity:** the owners'' claim after liabilities.
- **Revenue:** income from normal business activity.

### Example
A shop sells goods for ₹10,000 that cost ₹6,000, and pays ₹1,000 rent. Its profit is ₹3,000. Accounting records each transaction so that this result can be reported and trusted.

### Key takeaways
- Accounting is often called the language of business.
- Reports must be reliable and comparable.
- Profit and cash are not the same thing.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Financial Accounting');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Accounting Principles and Concepts', 'yYX4bvQSqbo', '### Introduction
Accounting follows shared rules so that one company''s reports can be compared with another''s. These are set out in frameworks such as GAAP and IFRS.

### Key concepts
- **Business entity:** the business is separate from its owner.
- **Going concern:** assume the business will continue operating.
- **Accrual:** record items when earned or incurred, not when cash moves.
- **Matching:** record expenses in the same period as related revenue.
- **Consistency and prudence:** use the same methods, and don''t overstate results.

### Definitions
- **GAAP:** Generally Accepted Accounting Principles.
- **IFRS:** International Financial Reporting Standards.
- **Accrual basis:** recording when the transaction occurs.
- **Materiality:** whether an item is large enough to affect decisions.

### Example
A firm completes work in March and gets paid in April. On the accrual basis, the revenue belongs to March, even though the cash arrives in April.

### Key takeaways
- Keep personal and business finances separate.
- Accrual gives a truer picture of performance than cash timing.
- Consistent methods make results comparable across years.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Accounting Principles and Concepts');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Accounting Equation and Double Entry System', 'kwA_6MV8dFE', '### Introduction
All of accounting rests on one equation. Double entry keeps it balanced by recording each transaction in two places.

### Key concepts
- **Accounting equation:** Assets = Liabilities + Equity.
- **Double entry:** every transaction has equal debits and credits.
- **Debit rules:** increase assets and expenses.
- **Credit rules:** increase liabilities, equity and revenue.

### Definitions
- **Debit (Dr):** the left side of an account.
- **Credit (Cr):** the right side of an account.
- **Account:** a record of one item, such as Cash.
- **Capital:** money invested by the owner.

### Example
An owner invests ₹50,000 in cash.
- Cash (an asset) increases by ₹50,000: debit Cash.
- Capital (equity) increases by ₹50,000: credit Capital.

Assets of 50,000 = Liabilities of 0 + Equity of 50,000. The equation balances.

### Key takeaways
- Total debits must always equal total credits.
- Debit and credit mean left and right, not good and bad.
- Every transaction affects at least two accounts.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Accounting Equation and Double Entry System');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Journal Entries and Ledger Posting', 'J7ntz3YSzY0', '### Introduction
Transactions are first recorded in date order in the journal, and then transferred to individual accounts in the ledger. This gives both a timeline and a running balance for each account.

### Key concepts
- **Journal:** the book of original entry, in date order.
- **Ledger:** groups all entries by account.
- **Posting:** moving journal entries to the ledger.
- **Balancing:** finding each account''s closing balance.

### Definitions
- **Journal entry:** a record of the accounts debited and credited.
- **Narration:** a short description of the transaction.
- **T-account:** a simple form showing debits on the left and credits on the right.

### Example
Bought furniture for ₹20,000 in cash.
```text
Furniture A/c   Dr   20,000
    To Cash A/c            20,000
(Being furniture purchased for cash)
```
This is then posted to the Furniture and Cash ledger accounts.

### Key takeaways
- The journal shows what happened, and when.
- The ledger shows the total position of each account.
- Clear narrations make records easy to check later.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Journal Entries and Ledger Posting');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Trial Balance and Rectification of Errors', 'w3hhhQxHiwo', '### Introduction
A trial balance lists all ledger balances to check that debits equal credits. It catches many mistakes, but not all of them.

### Key concepts
- **Trial balance:** a statement of all account balances at a date.
- **Errors it reveals:** one-sided entries or wrong totals.
- **Errors it misses:** omitted transactions, wrong accounts, or compensating errors.
- **Rectification:** correcting errors with new entries, not by erasing.

### Definitions
- **Error of omission:** a transaction not recorded at all.
- **Error of commission:** a wrong amount or the wrong person''s account.
- **Error of principle:** treating an item in the wrong category.
- **Suspense account:** temporarily holds a difference until it''s found.

### Example
A ₹5,000 machine purchase is recorded as a repair expense. Debits still equal credits, so the trial balance agrees. This error of principle is corrected by debiting Machinery and crediting Repairs.

### Key takeaways
- An agreed trial balance doesn''t prove the books are correct.
- Correct errors with entries, so there''s a clear record.
- Prepare a trial balance before the final accounts.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Trial Balance and Rectification of Errors');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Depreciation and Valuation of Assets', '_pas1ETbrj8', '### Introduction
Long-term assets wear out or become outdated. Depreciation spreads an asset''s cost over the years it is used, which matches expense to the revenue it helps earn.

### Key concepts
- **Causes:** wear, passage of time and obsolescence.
- **Straight-line method:** the same charge each year.
- **Written-down value method:** a fixed percentage of the remaining value.
- **Book value:** cost minus accumulated depreciation.

### Definitions
- **Depreciation:** allocating an asset''s cost over its useful life.
- **Salvage value:** the estimated value at the end of its life.
- **Useful life:** how long the asset is expected to be used.

### Example
A machine costs ₹1,00,000, with a salvage value of ₹10,000 and a 5-year life.
```text
Annual depreciation = (1,00,000 - 10,000) / 5 = ₹18,000
```
After two years, its book value is ₹64,000.

### Key takeaways
- Depreciation allocates cost; it isn''t a cash payment.
- Land is generally not depreciated.
- Use a consistent method from year to year.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Depreciation and Valuation of Assets');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Preparation of Final Accounts', '4N0Dmzhi3Tw', '### Introduction
At the end of a period, ledger balances are turned into reports that show profit and financial position. These are known as the final accounts.

### Key concepts
- **Trading account:** finds gross profit.
- **Profit and loss account:** finds net profit.
- **Balance sheet:** shows assets, liabilities and capital on a date.
- **Adjustments:** closing stock, outstanding and prepaid expenses, depreciation.

### Definitions
- **Gross profit:** sales minus the cost of goods sold.
- **Net profit:** gross profit minus other expenses.
- **Closing stock:** unsold goods at period end.
- **Outstanding expense:** an expense incurred but not yet paid.

### Example
```text
Sales 5,00,000 - Cost of goods sold 3,00,000 = Gross profit 2,00,000
Gross profit 2,00,000 - Expenses 1,20,000    = Net profit 80,000
```
The ₹80,000 is added to capital on the balance sheet.

### Key takeaways
- The profit and loss account covers a period; the balance sheet shows one date.
- Adjustments ensure that each period carries its correct expenses.
- The balance sheet must balance.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Preparation of Final Accounts');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 8, 'Cash Flow Statements and Financial Reporting', 'DiVPAjgmnj0', '### Introduction
A company can report a profit and still run out of cash. The cash flow statement shows where cash really came from and where it went.

### Key concepts
- **Operating activities:** cash from day-to-day business.
- **Investing activities:** buying or selling long-term assets.
- **Financing activities:** loans, share issues and dividends.
- **Indirect method:** starts from net profit and adjusts for non-cash items.

### Definitions
- **Cash flow:** cash moving in or out.
- **Free cash flow:** operating cash minus capital spending.
- **Annual report:** a company''s yearly financial report.
- **Non-cash expense:** such as depreciation, which reduces profit but not cash.

### Example
A firm has a net profit of ₹50,000 and depreciation of ₹10,000, while customers owe ₹20,000 more than last year. Operating cash flow = 50,000 + 10,000 - 20,000 = ₹40,000.

### Key takeaways
- Profit is an opinion shaped by rules; cash is a fact.
- Healthy companies generate cash from operations.
- Read all three statements together for the full picture.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cash Flow Statements and Financial Reporting');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Financial Management', 'i0az1n2JXWE', '### Introduction
Financial management is about acquiring funds and using them wisely. While accounting reports what happened, finance decides what to do next.

### Key concepts
- **Three major decisions:** investment, financing and dividend.
- **Goal:** maximizing shareholder wealth rather than short-term profit alone.
- **Risk and return:** higher expected return generally comes with higher risk.
- **Role of the finance manager:** planning funds, controlling them and managing risk.

### Definitions
- **Wealth maximization:** increasing the long-term value of the owners'' stake.
- **Investment decision:** where to put the funds.
- **Financing decision:** how to raise the funds.
- **Dividend decision:** how much profit to distribute.

### Example
A company with ₹1 crore can choose to buy a machine (investment), raise extra money through a loan (financing), and pay part of its profit to shareholders (dividend).

### Key takeaways
- Long-term value matters more than one year''s profit.
- Every financial decision balances risk and return.
- Finance depends on accounting information.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Financial Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Financial Planning and Forecasting', 'tYPknBUufVk', '### Introduction
Financial planning sets out how much money a business will need and where it will come from. Forecasting estimates the future so that surprises are smaller.

### Key concepts
- **Sales forecast:** usually the starting point.
- **Percent-of-sales method:** projects costs and assets from sales.
- **Budgets:** turn plans into numbers.
- **Scenario analysis:** tests best, likely and worst cases.

### Definitions
- **Forecast:** an estimate of a future result.
- **Budget:** a financial plan for a period.
- **Variance:** the difference between planned and actual results.
- **External financing needed:** funds required beyond what the business generates.

### Example
Sales are expected to grow from ₹10 lakh to ₹12 lakh, and costs are about 70% of sales. Forecast costs are ₹8.4 lakh, leaving ₹3.6 lakh before other expenses.

### Key takeaways
- A forecast is always an estimate, so review it regularly.
- Compare actual results with budgets, and investigate the differences.
- Plan for bad scenarios while there is still time to act.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Financial Planning and Forecasting');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Time Value of Money', '-OinhNhIMG0', '### Introduction
Money today is worth more than the same amount in the future, because it can earn a return. This idea underlies nearly every financial decision.

### Key concepts
- **Future value:** what money grows to.
- **Present value:** what future money is worth today.
- **Compounding:** earning interest on interest.
- **Discounting:** the reverse of compounding.
- **Annuities:** a series of equal payments.

### Definitions
- **Interest rate:** the return per period.
- **Discount rate:** the rate used to find present value.
- **Compound interest:** interest added to the principal.

### Example
```text
FV = PV x (1 + r)^n
₹10,000 at 10% for 2 years = 10,000 x 1.21 = ₹12,100
PV of ₹12,100 in 2 years at 10% = ₹10,000
```

### Key takeaways
- Only compare money amounts at the same point in time.
- Compounding grows powerful over long periods, so start early.
- A higher discount rate lowers present value.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Time Value of Money');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Capital Budgeting and Investment Decisions', 'NTHwQwDdQmA', '### Introduction
Capital budgeting evaluates big long-term projects, such as new factories. These decisions commit large sums and are hard to reverse.

### Key concepts
- **Payback period:** how long it takes to recover the investment.
- **NPV (Net Present Value):** present value of inflows minus the cost.
- **IRR (Internal Rate of Return):** the rate at which NPV equals zero.
- **Decision rules:** accept if NPV is positive, or if IRR exceeds the required return.

### Definitions
- **Cash inflow:** money the project brings in.
- **Hurdle rate:** the minimum acceptable return.
- **Mutually exclusive projects:** choosing one rules out the others.

### Example
A project costs ₹1,00,000 and returns ₹60,000 per year for two years. At 10%:
```text
NPV = 60,000/1.1 + 60,000/1.21 - 1,00,000 = about ₹4,132
```
NPV is positive, so accept.

### Key takeaways
- NPV is generally the most reliable measure.
- Payback ignores what happens after the recovery point.
- Use cash flows, not accounting profits.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Capital Budgeting and Investment Decisions');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Cost of Capital and Capital Structure', 'Yvi2f-AEuCM', '### Introduction
Funds aren''t free. The cost of capital is the return investors require, and capital structure is the mix of debt and equity used to raise it.

### Key concepts
- **Cost of debt:** interest, reduced by tax savings.
- **Cost of equity:** the return shareholders expect.
- **WACC:** the weighted average of these costs.
- **Financial leverage:** debt magnifies both profits and losses.
- **Optimal structure:** the mix that minimizes WACC.

### Definitions
- **Debt:** borrowed money with fixed obligations.
- **Equity:** owners'' funds.
- **WACC:** Weighted Average Cost of Capital.
- **Tax shield:** tax saved because interest is deductible.

### Example
With 60% equity at 12%, and 40% debt costing 8% after tax:
```text
WACC = 0.6 x 12% + 0.4 x 8% = 7.2% + 3.2% = 10.4%
```
Projects should earn more than 10.4%.

### Key takeaways
- WACC is a common hurdle rate for projects.
- Debt is often cheaper but adds risk.
- Too much debt can threaten a company''s survival.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cost of Capital and Capital Structure');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Working Capital Management', '9VSqG4CfrfM', '### Introduction
Working capital is what keeps daily operations running. Even profitable companies can fail if they can''t pay their short-term bills.

### Key concepts
- **Net working capital:** current assets minus current liabilities.
- **Operating cycle:** from buying stock to collecting cash.
- **Managing inventory:** enough to meet demand without tying up funds.
- **Managing receivables and payables:** collect sooner and pay on agreed terms.

### Definitions
- **Current assets:** cash, stock and receivables.
- **Current liabilities:** amounts due within a year.
- **Liquidity:** the ability to pay short-term obligations.
- **Current ratio:** current assets divided by current liabilities.

### Example
A store has ₹5 lakh of current assets and ₹2.5 lakh of current liabilities. Its current ratio is 2, and its net working capital is ₹2.5 lakh.

### Key takeaways
- Too little working capital risks being unable to pay bills.
- Too much ties up money that could be earning a return.
- Shorter operating cycles free up cash.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Working Capital Management');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Financial Risk Management and Analysis', 'R6QKBVwIB78', '### Introduction
Businesses face uncertain interest rates, currencies and customers. Risk management identifies these exposures and decides what to do about them, while ratio analysis helps to monitor financial health.

### Key concepts
- **Market risk:** changes in prices, interest rates and exchange rates.
- **Credit risk:** customers or borrowers failing to pay.
- **Liquidity risk:** not having cash when needed.
- **Responses:** avoid, reduce, transfer or accept.
- **Ratio analysis:** liquidity, profitability and leverage measures.

### Definitions
- **Hedging:** reducing risk using an offsetting position.
- **Diversification:** spreading investments to reduce risk.
- **Derivative:** a contract whose value depends on an underlying asset.

### Example
An Indian exporter expects US dollars in three months and fears the rupee will strengthen. A forward contract fixes the exchange rate today, which removes the uncertainty.

### Key takeaways
- Not all risks can or should be removed.
- Diversification reduces risk that''s specific to one investment.
- Review ratios over time and against similar companies.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Financial Management'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Financial Risk Management and Analysis');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Human Anatomy and Physiology', 'uBGl2BujkPQ', '### Introduction
Anatomy studies the body''s structures, and physiology studies how they work. The two are closely linked, because a structure''s form relates to its function.

### Key concepts
- **Levels of organization:** chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organism.
- **Homeostasis:** keeping internal conditions stable.
- **Anatomical position:** the standard reference posture.
- **Directional terms:** superior, inferior, anterior, posterior, medial and lateral.

### Definitions
- **Anatomy:** the study of body structure.
- **Physiology:** the study of body function.
- **Homeostasis:** maintaining a stable internal environment.
- **Negative feedback:** a response that reverses a change.

### Example
When body temperature rises, sensors signal the brain, which triggers sweating and widens skin blood vessels. Cooling then brings the temperature back down. This is negative feedback.

### Key takeaways
- Structure and function always go together.
- Most physiological control works by negative feedback.
- Learning directional terms makes all later anatomy easier.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Human Anatomy and Physiology');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Cells, Tissues and Body Organization', 'MB6mE6weCS4', '### Introduction
The cell is the basic unit of life. Similar cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs work together in systems.

### Key concepts
- **Cell parts:** membrane, nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes.
- **Four tissue types:** epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous.
- **Organs:** contain several tissue types working together.
- **Body cavities:** spaces that protect internal organs.

### Definitions
- **Tissue:** a group of similar cells with a shared function.
- **Organ:** a structure made of different tissues.
- **Organelle:** a specialized structure within a cell.
- **Mitochondria:** produce most of the cell''s energy (ATP).

### Example
The stomach is an organ: epithelial tissue lines it and secretes digestive juices, muscle tissue churns food, connective tissue holds it together, and nervous tissue controls its activity.

### Key takeaways
- Each level builds on the one below it.
- All four tissue types appear in most organs.
- A cell''s shape reflects its job.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cells, Tissues and Body Organization');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Skeletal and Muscular Systems', '656ZfzFD-ec', '### Introduction
Bones and muscles work together to support the body and allow movement. The adult human skeleton has 206 bones and hundreds of skeletal muscles.

### Key concepts
- **Skeleton functions:** support, protection, movement, mineral storage, blood cell production.
- **Divisions:** axial (skull, spine, ribs) and appendicular (limbs, girdles).
- **Joints:** where bones meet.
- **Muscle types:** skeletal, cardiac and smooth.
- **Antagonistic pairs:** muscles pull but cannot push.

### Definitions
- **Tendon:** connects muscle to bone.
- **Ligament:** connects bone to bone.
- **Cartilage:** flexible cushioning tissue.
- **Sarcomere:** the basic contracting unit of a muscle.

### Example
Bending your elbow: the biceps contracts while the triceps relaxes. Straightening it reverses this. Because muscles only pull, movement requires these opposing pairs.

### Key takeaways
- Red bone marrow makes blood cells.
- Skeletal muscle is voluntary; cardiac and smooth muscle are involuntary.
- Weight-bearing exercise helps to strengthen bones.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Skeletal and Muscular Systems');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Nervous System and Sense Organs', 'RNLceVI8jcc', '### Introduction
The nervous system is the body''s fast communication network. It senses changes, processes information and directs responses.

### Key concepts
- **Central nervous system:** the brain and spinal cord.
- **Peripheral nervous system:** nerves throughout the body.
- **Neurons:** carry electrical signals.
- **Synapses:** chemical signalling between neurons.
- **Senses:** sight, hearing, taste, smell and touch.

### Definitions
- **Neuron:** a nerve cell.
- **Neurotransmitter:** a chemical that crosses a synapse.
- **Reflex:** a fast automatic response.
- **Autonomic nervous system:** controls involuntary functions such as heart rate.

### Example
If you touch something hot, sensory neurons signal the spinal cord, which immediately activates motor neurons to pull your hand away. You feel the pain only after the reflex has begun.

### Key takeaways
- Signals are electrical along a neuron and chemical across a synapse.
- Reflexes protect you by bypassing the brain''s decision making.
- Sympathetic means "fight or flight"; parasympathetic means "rest and digest".', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Nervous System and Sense Organs');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Cardiovascular and Respiratory Systems', '9fxm85Fy4sQ', '### Introduction
These two systems work as a team. The lungs bring in oxygen and remove carbon dioxide, while the heart and blood vessels carry those gases around the body.

### Key concepts
- **Heart:** four chambers with two atria and two ventricles.
- **Double circulation:** pulmonary (to the lungs) and systemic (to the body).
- **Blood vessels:** arteries, veins and capillaries.
- **Gas exchange:** occurs in the alveoli.
- **Breathing:** driven by the diaphragm.

### Definitions
- **Artery:** carries blood away from the heart.
- **Vein:** carries blood toward the heart.
- **Alveoli:** tiny air sacs in the lungs.
- **Haemoglobin:** the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells.

### Example
Blood returns from the body to the right heart, goes to the lungs to pick up oxygen, returns to the left heart, and is then pumped through the aorta to the body.

### Key takeaways
- Valves stop blood from flowing backward.
- Exchange with tissues happens through thin capillary walls.
- The pulmonary artery is unusual: it carries oxygen-poor blood.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cardiovascular and Respiratory Systems');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Digestive System and Nutrition', 'yIoTRGfcMqM', '### Introduction
The digestive system breaks food down into molecules that the body can absorb and use. Nutrition is about which of those molecules the body needs.

### Key concepts
- **Path of food:** mouth, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine.
- **Accessory organs:** liver, gallbladder and pancreas.
- **Enzymes:** chemically break food down.
- **Absorption:** most occurs in the small intestine.
- **Macronutrients:** carbohydrates, proteins and fats.

### Definitions
- **Peristalsis:** muscle waves that move food along.
- **Enzyme:** a protein that speeds up a reaction.
- **Villi:** finger-like folds that increase intestinal surface area.
- **Bile:** a fluid from the liver that helps digest fats.

### Example
Starch begins breaking down in the mouth through salivary amylase. Protein digestion starts in the acidic stomach. Most digestion finishes in the small intestine, where nutrients enter the blood.

### Key takeaways
- The small intestine does most of the absorption.
- The large intestine mainly absorbs water.
- A balanced diet includes macronutrients, vitamins, minerals and fibre.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Digestive System and Nutrition');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Excretory and Urinary Systems', 'q5qaGHfdmYM', '### Introduction
Metabolism produces waste that must be removed. The urinary system filters blood and also controls the body''s water and salt balance.

### Key concepts
- **Urinary organs:** kidneys, ureters, bladder and urethra.
- **Nephron:** the kidney''s functional unit.
- **Urine formation:** filtration, reabsorption and secretion.
- **Other excretory organs:** lungs, skin and liver.

### Definitions
- **Nephron:** a tiny filtering unit in the kidney.
- **Urea:** a nitrogen waste product made in the liver.
- **Glomerulus:** the capillary network where filtration starts.
- **ADH:** a hormone that helps the kidneys retain water.

### Example
When you are dehydrated, ADH is released and the kidneys reabsorb more water. Your urine becomes darker and more concentrated, which conserves water in the body.

### Key takeaways
- Each kidney contains about a million nephrons.
- Most filtered water is reabsorbed back into the blood.
- Kidneys also help control blood pressure.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Excretory and Urinary Systems');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 8, 'Endocrine and Reproductive Systems', 'eWHH9je2zG4', '### Introduction
The endocrine system controls the body using chemical messengers carried in the blood. Its hormones regulate growth, metabolism and reproduction.

### Key concepts
- **Major glands:** pituitary, thyroid, adrenal and pancreas.
- **Hormone action:** affects only cells with matching receptors.
- **Feedback loops:** keep hormone levels in range.
- **Reproductive organs:** testes and ovaries produce sex cells and hormones.

### Definitions
- **Hormone:** a chemical messenger carried in the blood.
- **Gland:** an organ that secretes substances.
- **Insulin:** lowers blood sugar.
- **Gonads:** the testes and ovaries.

### Example
After a meal, blood sugar rises and the pancreas releases insulin, which helps cells take in glucose. Between meals, glucagon signals the liver to release stored glucose.

### Key takeaways
- Nerve signals are fast and brief; hormones are slower and last longer.
- The pituitary is often called the "master gland".
- Hormones such as estrogen and testosterone drive reproductive development.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Human Anatomy & Physiology'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Endocrine and Reproductive Systems');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Genetics and Heredity', '2reevjoVn6c', '### Introduction
Genetics is the study of how traits pass from parents to offspring and why individuals differ. It explains everything from eye colour to many inherited diseases.

### Key concepts
- **Heredity:** the passing of traits between generations.
- **Variation:** differences among individuals.
- **Genes and environment:** traits can be influenced by both.
- **Genotype and phenotype:** genetic makeup versus observable traits.

### Definitions
- **Gene:** a section of DNA that codes for a product.
- **Allele:** a version of a gene.
- **Trait:** a characteristic, such as height.
- **Genome:** an organism''s complete DNA.

### Example
Height is strongly influenced by many genes, but nutrition during childhood also matters. So two people with similar genes can reach different heights.

### Key takeaways
- You inherit half your DNA from each parent.
- Phenotype results from genes interacting with the environment.
- Variation is what makes evolution possible.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Genetics and Heredity');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Cell Structure and Chromosomes', 'BbA-pHyirzo', '### Introduction
Genetic information is stored inside the cell and packaged into chromosomes. This packaging lets very long DNA molecules fit into a tiny nucleus and divide accurately.

### Key concepts
- **Nucleus:** holds DNA in eukaryotic cells.
- **Chromosome structure:** DNA wrapped around histone proteins.
- **Human chromosomes:** 23 pairs, or 46 in total.
- **Mitosis:** makes identical body cells.
- **Meiosis:** makes sex cells with half the chromosomes.

### Definitions
- **Chromatin:** DNA and protein in the nucleus.
- **Centromere:** the point joining two sister chromatids.
- **Homologous chromosomes:** a matching pair, one from each parent.
- **Karyotype:** an image of a cell''s chromosomes.

### Example
Sex chromosomes form the 23rd pair: XX is typically female and XY typically male. Each egg carries an X, and each sperm carries an X or a Y.

### Key takeaways
- Body cells are diploid (46); sex cells are haploid (23).
- Fertilization restores the full chromosome count.
- Meiosis shuffles genes and so creates variation.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Cell Structure and Chromosomes');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'DNA and RNA Structure', 'JQByjprj_mA', '### Introduction
DNA and RNA are the molecules of genetic information. Their structure explains how information is stored, copied and used.

### Key concepts
- **Nucleotides:** a sugar, a phosphate and a base.
- **DNA:** a double helix with bases A, T, G and C.
- **Base pairing:** A with T, and G with C.
- **RNA:** single-stranded, using U in place of T.
- **Types of RNA:** mRNA, tRNA and rRNA.

### Definitions
- **Nucleotide:** the building block of nucleic acids.
- **Double helix:** the twisted-ladder shape of DNA.
- **Complementary strands:** strands that match by base pairing.
- **Deoxyribose:** the sugar in DNA.

### Example
If one DNA strand reads `ATGC`, its complementary strand reads `TACG`. An RNA copy of the strand `ATGC` reads `UACG`, because RNA uses U in place of T.

### Key takeaways
- DNA stores information, while RNA helps to use it.
- Base pairing allows accurate copying.
- Hydrogen bonds hold the two DNA strands together.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'DNA and RNA Structure');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'DNA Replication and Protein Synthesis', '6gUY5NoX1Lk', '### Introduction
Cells must copy DNA before dividing, and they read it to build proteins. This flow of information, from DNA to RNA to protein, is called the central dogma.

### Key concepts
- **Replication:** copies DNA in a semi-conservative way.
- **Transcription:** copies a gene into mRNA in the nucleus.
- **Translation:** ribosomes build a protein from mRNA.
- **Codons:** three-base sequences that specify amino acids.

### Definitions
- **DNA polymerase:** the enzyme that builds new DNA strands.
- **Helicase:** the enzyme that unwinds DNA.
- **RNA polymerase:** the enzyme that makes RNA.
- **Codon:** three bases coding for one amino acid.

### Example
The mRNA codon `AUG` codes for methionine and also signals the start of a protein. A tRNA carrying methionine pairs with it, and the ribosome adds the following amino acids.

### Key takeaways
- Each new DNA molecule keeps one original strand.
- Transcription occurs in the nucleus, and translation in the cytoplasm.
- Proteins carry out most of the work in a cell.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'DNA Replication and Protein Synthesis');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Mendelian Genetics and Inheritance', '3f_eisNPpnc', '### Introduction
Gregor Mendel''s pea plant experiments revealed the basic rules of inheritance. His findings still let us predict how traits are passed on.

### Key concepts
- **Law of segregation:** allele pairs separate when sex cells form.
- **Law of independent assortment:** different genes are inherited independently.
- **Dominant and recessive alleles:** dominant alleles mask recessive ones.
- **Punnett squares:** predict the offspring''s chances.

### Definitions
- **Homozygous:** two identical alleles.
- **Heterozygous:** two different alleles.
- **Genotype:** the allele combination, such as `Tt`.
- **Phenotype:** the visible trait, such as tall.

### Example
Crossing two `Tt` tall plants gives:
```text
TT : Tt : tt = 1 : 2 : 1
Tall : Short  = 3 : 1
```
Each offspring has a 25% chance of being short.

### Key takeaways
- A recessive trait shows only when both alleles are recessive.
- Punnett squares give probabilities, not guarantees.
- Many human traits involve multiple genes and don''t follow simple ratios.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Mendelian Genetics and Inheritance');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Mutations and Genetic Disorders', 'vl6Vlf2thvI', '### Introduction
A mutation is a change in DNA. Most have little effect, and some are harmful, but mutations are also the source of genetic variation.

### Key concepts
- **Point mutations:** a single base changes.
- **Frameshift mutations:** adding or removing bases shifts the reading frame.
- **Chromosomal mutations:** large sections or whole chromosomes change.
- **Causes:** copying errors, radiation and certain chemicals.
- **Inheritance:** germline mutations can pass to children; somatic ones don''t.

### Definitions
- **Mutation:** a change in DNA sequence.
- **Mutagen:** something that causes mutations.
- **Germline cell:** a sex cell or its precursor.
- **Carrier:** someone with one copy of a recessive disease allele.

### Example
Sickle cell disease is caused by a single base change in the haemoglobin gene. Down syndrome results from an extra copy of chromosome 21, which is a chromosomal change.

### Key takeaways
- Not all mutations are harmful.
- Carriers often have no symptoms but can pass the allele on.
- Genetic counselling helps families understand their risks.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Mutations and Genetic Disorders');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Modern Genetics and Genetic Engineering', 'CDw4WPng2iE', '### Introduction
Scientists can now read and edit DNA directly. These tools are transforming medicine and agriculture while raising serious ethical questions.

### Key concepts
- **Recombinant DNA:** combining DNA from different sources.
- **PCR:** makes millions of copies of a DNA region.
- **CRISPR-Cas9:** a precise gene-editing tool.
- **Gene therapy:** treats disease by changing genes.
- **DNA sequencing:** reads the order of bases.

### Definitions
- **Genetic engineering:** directly changing an organism''s DNA.
- **Plasmid:** a small circular DNA in bacteria, used to carry genes.
- **GMO:** a genetically modified organism.
- **Restriction enzyme:** cuts DNA at specific sequences.

### Example
Human insulin is produced by placing the human insulin gene into bacteria using a plasmid. The bacteria multiply and make insulin, which is then purified and used to treat diabetes.

### Key takeaways
- Genetic engineering already produces medicines and improved crops.
- CRISPR makes editing cheaper and more precise.
- Society must consider safety, privacy and fairness.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Introduction to Genetics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Modern Genetics and Genetic Engineering');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Physics and Measurement', '2I8XMxMvWl0', '### Introduction
Physics studies matter, energy and how they interact. Because it relies on experiments, accurate measurement is its foundation.

### Key concepts
- **SI base units:** metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, candela.
- **Derived units:** such as newton and joule, built from base units.
- **Scalars and vectors:** size alone, or size and direction.
- **Significant figures:** show how precise a measurement is.

### Definitions
- **Physical quantity:** something that can be measured.
- **Accuracy:** closeness to the true value.
- **Precision:** how close repeated measurements are to each other.
- **Dimensional analysis:** checking equations using their units.

### Example
Speed is measured in metres per second. To convert 72 km/h:
```text
72 km/h = 72 x 1000 m / 3600 s = 20 m/s
```

### Key takeaways
- Always include units with a value.
- Quantities can be added only if they have the same units.
- A measurement can be precise without being accurate.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Physics and Measurement');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Motion and Laws of Motion', 'g550H4e5FCY', '### Introduction
Kinematics describes how objects move, and Newton''s laws explain why. Together they can predict the motion of nearly everything in daily life.

### Key concepts
- **First law:** an object keeps its motion unless a net force acts (inertia).
- **Second law:** F = ma.
- **Third law:** every action has an equal and opposite reaction.
- **Equations of motion:** connect velocity, acceleration, time and distance.

### Definitions
- **Velocity:** speed in a given direction.
- **Acceleration:** the rate of change of velocity.
- **Force:** a push or pull, measured in newtons.
- **Inertia:** resistance to a change in motion.

### Example
A 1000 kg car accelerates at 2 m/s².
```text
F = m x a = 1000 x 2 = 2000 N
```
A car starting at rest reaches v = at = 2 x 5 = 10 m/s after 5 seconds.

### Key takeaways
- Motion doesn''t need a force; a change in motion does.
- Action and reaction forces act on different objects.
- Mass isn''t weight: weight is the force of gravity on a mass.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Motion and Laws of Motion');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Work, Energy and Power', 'w4QFJb9a8vo', '### Introduction
Energy lets things happen, and it transforms between forms but is never destroyed. Work transfers energy, and power tells how fast that happens.

### Key concepts
- **Work:** W = F d cos(theta).
- **Kinetic energy:** KE = 1/2 m v².
- **Potential energy:** PE = m g h.
- **Conservation of energy:** the total energy stays constant.
- **Power:** P = W / t.

### Definitions
- **Joule:** the unit of work and energy.
- **Watt:** the unit of power, one joule per second.
- **Efficiency:** useful energy out divided by energy in.

### Example
A 2 kg ball falls from 5 m, ignoring air resistance (g = 10 m/s²):
```text
PE = 2 x 10 x 5 = 100 J
At the ground: 1/2 x 2 x v² = 100, so v = 10 m/s
```

### Key takeaways
- No work is done if there''s no movement along the force.
- Energy changes form, often into heat.
- Doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times larger.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Work, Energy and Power');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Gravitation', 'HjKL7R-aTJE', '### Introduction
Gravity pulls every mass toward every other mass. The same force that makes an apple fall also keeps the planets in orbit.

### Key concepts
- **Newton''s law of gravitation:** F = G m1 m2 / r².
- **Inverse square law:** doubling the distance makes the force four times weaker.
- **Gravitational field:** the force per unit mass around an object.
- **Orbits:** gravity provides the required centripetal force.

### Definitions
- **G:** the gravitational constant, 6.67 x 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg².
- **g:** acceleration due to gravity, about 9.8 m/s² on Earth.
- **Weight:** W = m g.
- **Escape velocity:** the speed required to leave a body''s gravity.

### Example
A 60 kg person has a weight of about 588 N on Earth. On the Moon, where g is about 1.6 m/s², they weigh about 96 N, but their mass is still 60 kg.

### Key takeaways
- Mass doesn''t change between planets, but weight does.
- Without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate.
- Astronauts in orbit are in continuous free fall, which is why they float.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Gravitation');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Properties of Matter and Fluids', 'kkq8ruV8_Jw', '### Introduction
Materials respond to forces in different ways, and liquids and gases flow. These properties explain why ships float and how hydraulic brakes work.

### Key concepts
- **Density:** mass per unit volume.
- **Pressure:** P = F / A.
- **Pascal''s principle:** pressure on a confined fluid spreads equally.
- **Archimedes'' principle:** buoyant force equals the weight of fluid displaced.
- **Bernoulli''s principle:** faster-moving fluid has lower pressure.

### Definitions
- **Elasticity:** returning to shape after a force is removed.
- **Viscosity:** a fluid''s resistance to flow.
- **Buoyancy:** the upward force from a fluid.
- **Surface tension:** the tendency of a liquid''s surface to resist force.

### Example
Pressure in a liquid increases with depth:
```text
P = density x g x depth
At 10 m in water: 1000 x 10 x 10 = 100,000 Pa
```

### Key takeaways
- Sharp objects cut well because small area means high pressure.
- An object floats if it''s less dense overall than the fluid.
- Hydraulic systems multiply force using Pascal''s principle.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Properties of Matter and Fluids');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Heat and Thermodynamics', 'LMVIVwD6s2s', '### Introduction
Thermodynamics studies heat, temperature and energy transfer. It explains engines and refrigerators, and why some processes only happen in one direction.

### Key concepts
- **Temperature and heat:** temperature measures particle motion; heat is energy in transfer.
- **Heat transfer:** conduction, convection and radiation.
- **First law:** energy is conserved.
- **Second law:** heat flows naturally from hot to cold, and entropy tends to increase.

### Definitions
- **Specific heat capacity:** the energy needed to warm 1 kg by 1 °C.
- **Latent heat:** energy absorbed or released in a change of state.
- **Entropy:** a measure of disorder.

### Example
Heating 2 kg of water by 10 °C (specific heat is about 4200 J/kg °C):
```text
Q = m c delta-T = 2 x 4200 x 10 = 84,000 J
```

### Key takeaways
- Heat and temperature are different things.
- No heat engine can be 100% efficient.
- Water''s high specific heat helps to stabilize climates.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Heat and Thermodynamics');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Waves and Sound', 'Y1LbQBgvISs', '### Introduction
Waves carry energy from one place to another without carrying matter along with them. Sound is a mechanical wave that needs a medium to travel through.

### Key concepts
- **Transverse waves:** vibrations perpendicular to travel.
- **Longitudinal waves:** vibrations parallel to travel, such as sound.
- **Wave equation:** v = f x wavelength.
- **Sound properties:** pitch depends on frequency, and loudness on amplitude.
- **Doppler effect:** pitch changes as a source moves.

### Definitions
- **Frequency:** waves per second, measured in hertz.
- **Wavelength:** the distance between wave peaks.
- **Amplitude:** the maximum displacement.
- **Echo:** a reflected sound.

### Example
Sound travels at about 340 m/s in air. A 170 Hz tone has:
```text
wavelength = v / f = 340 / 170 = 2 m
```

### Key takeaways
- Sound can''t travel through a vacuum.
- Sound travels faster in solids than in air.
- A passing ambulance''s pitch drops because of the Doppler effect.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Waves and Sound');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 8, 'Electricity and Magnetism', 's94suB5uLWw', '### Introduction
Electricity and magnetism are two aspects of one force. Their connection makes motors, generators and the power grid possible.

### Key concepts
- **Ohm''s law:** V = I R.
- **Circuits:** series and parallel.
- **Electric power:** P = V I.
- **Magnetic fields:** produced by moving charges.
- **Electromagnetic induction:** a changing magnetic field creates a current.

### Definitions
- **Current:** the flow of charge, measured in amperes.
- **Voltage:** the energy per charge, measured in volts.
- **Resistance:** opposition to current, measured in ohms.
- **Transformer:** changes the voltage of alternating current.

### Example
A 12 V battery connected to a 4 ohm resistor:
```text
I = V / R = 12 / 4 = 3 A
P = V x I = 12 x 3 = 36 W
```

### Key takeaways
- In series, components share voltage; in parallel, they share current.
- Motors turn electricity into motion; generators do the reverse.
- Household appliances are wired in parallel, so each gets full voltage.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Fundamentals of Physics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Electricity and Magnetism');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Fundamentals of Algebra', 'NybHckSEQBI', '### Introduction
Algebra uses letters to stand for unknown or changing values. It turns real-world situations into equations that can be solved systematically.

### Key concepts
- **Variables and expressions:** `3x + 2` combines numbers and letters.
- **Solving equations:** do the same operation to both sides.
- **Order of operations:** brackets, powers, multiplication and division, addition and subtraction.
- **Factoring:** rewriting an expression as a product.

### Definitions
- **Variable:** a symbol standing for a number.
- **Coefficient:** the number multiplying a variable.
- **Equation:** a statement that two expressions are equal.
- **Linear equation:** an equation whose variable has a power of 1.

### Example
Solve `2x + 6 = 14`:
```text
2x = 14 - 6 = 8
x = 8 / 2 = 4
```
Check: 2(4) + 6 = 14.

### Key takeaways
- Whatever you do to one side, do to the other.
- Always check your answer by substituting it back.
- Only like terms can be combined.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Fundamentals of Algebra');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Functions and Graphs', '52tpYl2tTqk', '### Introduction
A function describes how one quantity depends on another. Graphs show that relationship visually, making patterns easy to see.

### Key concepts
- **Function:** each input gives exactly one output.
- **Domain and range:** allowed inputs and resulting outputs.
- **Linear functions:** y = mx + c, a straight line.
- **Quadratic functions:** y = ax² + bx + c, a parabola.
- **Vertical line test:** checks whether a graph is a function.

### Definitions
- **Slope (m):** the rate of change of a line.
- **Intercept:** where a graph crosses an axis.
- **Domain:** the set of possible inputs.
- **Range:** the set of possible outputs.

### Example
A taxi charges ₹50 plus ₹15 per kilometre:
```text
f(d) = 15d + 50
f(10) = 15(10) + 50 = ₹200
```

### Key takeaways
- `f(x)` means the output of f for the input x.
- Slope tells you how fast something is changing.
- Graphing a function quickly reveals its behaviour.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Functions and Graphs');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Differential Calculus', 'WsQQvHm4lSw', '### Introduction
Differential calculus studies how quantities change. The derivative gives an instantaneous rate of change, such as a car''s speed at one exact moment.

### Key concepts
- **Limits:** the value a function approaches.
- **Derivative:** the slope of a function at a point.
- **Rules:** power, product, quotient and chain rules.
- **Optimization:** a maximum or minimum occurs where the derivative is zero.

### Definitions
- **Derivative:** the instantaneous rate of change, written `dy/dx`.
- **Tangent:** a line touching a curve at a point.
- **Critical point:** where the derivative is zero or undefined.

### Example
If position is `s = t²`, then velocity is its derivative:
```text
ds/dt = 2t
At t = 3 seconds, velocity = 6 m/s
```
Power rule: the derivative of xⁿ is n xⁿ⁻¹.

### Key takeaways
- A derivative is a slope and a rate of change.
- Set the derivative to zero to find maximums and minimums.
- Velocity is the derivative of position, and acceleration of velocity.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Differential Calculus');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Integral Calculus', 'tB3wJ-bwdZc', '### Introduction
Integration is the reverse of differentiation. It adds up tiny pieces to find totals, such as the area under a curve or the distance travelled.

### Key concepts
- **Indefinite integral:** a family of functions, plus a constant C.
- **Definite integral:** a number found between two limits.
- **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus:** links integrals and derivatives.
- **Techniques:** substitution and integration by parts.

### Definitions
- **Antiderivative:** a function whose derivative is the given function.
- **Constant of integration:** the unknown constant, C.
- **Limits of integration:** the start and end values.

### Example
```text
Integral of 2x dx = x² + C
From 0 to 3: 3² - 0² = 9
```
So the area under `y = 2x` from 0 to 3 is 9. Power rule: the integral of xⁿ is xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C.

### Key takeaways
- Don''t forget +C on indefinite integrals.
- A definite integral equals the upper-limit value minus the lower-limit value.
- Integrating velocity over time gives displacement.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Integral Calculus');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 5, 'Matrices and Determinants', 'ZFVFtVbeerI', '### Introduction
A matrix is a rectangular table of numbers. Matrices organize data and solve systems of equations, and they''re used heavily in graphics and machine learning.

### Key concepts
- **Order:** rows x columns.
- **Operations:** addition, and scalar and matrix multiplication.
- **Determinant:** a number computed from a square matrix.
- **Inverse:** exists only if the determinant isn''t zero.

### Definitions
- **Square matrix:** the same number of rows and columns.
- **Identity matrix:** 1s on the diagonal and 0s elsewhere.
- **Transpose:** rows and columns swapped.
- **Singular matrix:** a matrix whose determinant is zero.

### Example
```text
A = | 3  2 |
    | 1  4 |
det(A) = (3)(4) - (2)(1) = 10
```
Because the determinant isn''t zero, A has an inverse.

### Key takeaways
- Matrix multiplication isn''t commutative: AB usually differs from BA.
- To multiply, the columns of the first must equal the rows of the second.
- A zero determinant means no unique solution.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Matrices and Determinants');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 6, 'Probability and Statistics', '_Pyi12dn4Kw', '### Introduction
Statistics summarizes and interprets data, while probability measures uncertainty. Together they support decisions in science, business and machine learning.

### Key concepts
- **Averages:** mean, median and mode.
- **Spread:** range, variance and standard deviation.
- **Probability:** favourable outcomes divided by total outcomes.
- **Normal distribution:** the bell curve.
- **Correlation:** how two variables move together.

### Definitions
- **Population:** the entire group of interest.
- **Sample:** a subset of a population.
- **Independent events:** one doesn''t affect the other.
- **Outlier:** a value far from the rest.

### Example
Marks of 10, 20 and 90:
```text
Mean = 40, Median = 20
```
The outlier 90 pulls the mean up, so the median better represents a typical value. The probability of rolling a 6 is 1/6.

### Key takeaways
- Use the median when data contains outliers.
- Correlation doesn''t prove causation.
- A sample must be representative to be useful.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Probability and Statistics');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 7, 'Linear Algebra', '5oZ84mlt7tM', '### Introduction
Linear algebra studies vectors and the transformations that act on them. It''s the mathematical language of computer graphics, data science and machine learning.

### Key concepts
- **Vectors:** have magnitude and direction.
- **Dot product:** measures how aligned two vectors are.
- **Linear transformations:** matrices that rotate, scale or shear space.
- **Eigenvectors:** directions that a transformation only scales.
- **Systems of equations:** written as Ax = b.

### Definitions
- **Vector space:** a set of vectors closed under addition and scaling.
- **Basis:** the minimal set of vectors describing a space.
- **Rank:** the number of independent rows or columns.
- **Eigenvalue:** the factor by which an eigenvector is scaled.

### Example
```text
a = (1, 2), b = (3, 4)
a . b = (1)(3) + (2)(4) = 11
```
A dot product of zero means the vectors are perpendicular.

### Key takeaways
- A matrix is best thought of as a transformation of space.
- PCA in machine learning is built on eigenvectors.
- Visual understanding makes the formulas far easier.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Linear Algebra');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 8, 'Mathematical Applications and Problem Solving', 'yHYMArAVDWY', '### Introduction
Applied mathematics means turning real problems into mathematical models and interpreting the answers. A clear method matters as much as knowing formulas.

### Key concepts
- **Pólya''s four steps:** understand the problem, make a plan, carry it out, look back.
- **Mathematical modelling:** represent a situation using equations.
- **Assumptions:** simplify the problem, and state them clearly.
- **Estimation:** check that an answer is sensible.

### Definitions
- **Model:** a simplified mathematical description of a situation.
- **Variable:** a quantity that can change.
- **Optimization:** finding the best possible value.

### Example
A rectangular garden needs the largest area from 40 m of fencing. Using sides x and 20 - x:
```text
A = x(20 - x) = 20x - x²
dA/dx = 20 - 2x = 0, so x = 10
```
The best shape is a 10 m square, with an area of 100 m².

### Key takeaways
- Define your variables before calculating.
- Check units and whether the answer makes sense.
- Practise on a wide range of problems to build judgement.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Applied Mathematics'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Mathematical Applications and Problem Solving');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 1, 'Introduction to Graphic Design', 'dFSia1LZI4Y', '### Introduction
Graphic design is visual communication: combining images, type and layout to deliver a message. Unlike pure art, it''s made to solve a problem for a specific audience.

### Key concepts
- **Purpose:** to inform, persuade or guide.
- **Design fields:** branding, print, web and UI, advertising, motion.
- **Design process:** brief, research, sketch, design, feedback, refine.
- **Core tools:** Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, Figma and Canva.

### Definitions
- **Design brief:** a document stating a project''s goals and requirements.
- **Raster image:** made of pixels, such as a photo.
- **Vector image:** made of mathematical shapes; scales without quality loss.
- **Mockup:** a realistic preview of a design.

### Example
A brief for a college fest poster states the audience (students), the message (date and venue), and the goal (increase attendance). Each design decision then serves that goal.

### Key takeaways
- Good design communicates first, and decorates second.
- Always begin with the brief and the audience.
- Use vectors for logos and raster formats for photos.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Graphic Design Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Introduction to Graphic Design');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 2, 'Principles of Design and Composition', '9EPTM91TBDU', '### Introduction
Design principles are guidelines for arranging elements so that a layout is clear and appealing. They explain why some designs simply work better than others.

### Key concepts
- **Balance:** visual weight spread symmetrically or asymmetrically.
- **Contrast:** differences that attract attention.
- **Hierarchy:** the order in which things get noticed.
- **Alignment, repetition, proximity:** create order and consistency.
- **Rule of thirds:** place key elements along grid lines.

### Definitions
- **Composition:** the arrangement of elements on a page.
- **White space:** the empty area around elements.
- **Focal point:** where the eye goes first.
- **Grid:** invisible lines that organize a layout.

### Example
On an event poster, a very large title (hierarchy) in high contrast against the background catches the eye. The date and venue are grouped together (proximity), and everything lines up on a grid.

### Key takeaways
- White space is a tool, not wasted space.
- If everything is emphasized, nothing stands out.
- Squint at your design to test whether the hierarchy works.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Graphic Design Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Principles of Design and Composition');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 3, 'Color Theory and Typography', '_2LLXnUdUIc', '### Introduction
Colour sets the mood, and typography gives words a voice. Together they strongly shape how people feel about and read a design.

### Key concepts
- **Colour wheel:** primary, secondary and tertiary colours.
- **Harmonies:** complementary, analogous and triadic.
- **Colour models:** RGB for screens and CMYK for print.
- **Type classes:** serif, sans-serif, script and display.
- **Spacing:** kerning, leading and tracking.

### Definitions
- **Hue:** the colour itself.
- **Saturation:** the colour''s intensity.
- **Typeface:** a family of letter designs.
- **Leading:** the vertical space between lines of text.

### Example
A healthcare website might use calm blues with a clean sans-serif typeface to feel trustworthy. A pairing of one bold heading font and one readable body font usually works best.

### Key takeaways
- Limit designs to about two typefaces and a few colours.
- Ensure strong contrast between text and background.
- Colours carry cultural meanings, so consider your audience.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Graphic Design Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Color Theory and Typography');

INSERT INTO learnhub_course.course_modules
    (course_id, position, title, youtube_video_id, notes, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT c.id, 4, 'Image Editing Fundamentals', '80434t3GTgA', '### Introduction
Image editing improves photos and combines them into new designs. A few core skills cover most everyday needs.

### Key concepts
- **Layers:** separate elements stacked on top of each other.
- **Non-destructive editing:** masks and adjustment layers keep the original intact.
- **Adjustments:** brightness, contrast and colour balance.
- **Selections and masks:** isolate parts of an image.
- **File formats:** JPEG, PNG, SVG and PSD.

### Definitions
- **Resolution:** pixels per inch; 300 PPI is typical for print, and screens need less.
- **Layer mask:** hides parts of a layer without deleting them.
- **Crop:** trim the edges of an image.

### Example
To put a person on a new background: select the person, add a layer mask, place the background on a lower layer, and match the colours. Because a mask is used, you can still refine the edges later.

### Key takeaways
- Keep an editable original and work on copies.
- Use PNG for transparency and JPEG for photos.
- Only use images you have the right to use.', UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
FROM learnhub_course.courses c
WHERE c.title = 'Graphic Design Fundamentals'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM learnhub_course.course_modules m WHERE m.course_id = c.id AND m.title = 'Image Editing Fundamentals');

